                <pb facs="0110" n="26" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg26"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72114/3413</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stovall</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berne</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-20T17:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 20, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received March 23, 2.05 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2902. Information received from German Army administration, dated February 6,
                    through Spanish Embassy, Berlin, states captured soldiers and sailors can wear
                    either uniform provided by own Government, or clothing furnished for prisoners
                    by German Government, consisting of cap, coat, trousers, overcoat of dark color,
                    necktie, and footwear. Clothing must be marked with stripe of brood [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">khaki?</hi>] material 10 centimeters wide on left upper
                    arm of coat, stripe 5 centimeters wide on seams of trousers, and band on cap.
                    Military prisoners may also wear civilian clothing of black material sent from
                    home if materials as stated. Appropriate clothing provided for special
                    occupations. No particular uniform required for civilian prisoners. Every
                    prisoner wear own personal clothing. These regulations clothing noted in
                    connection with uniforms to be sent to Berne for American prisoners.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stovall</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
